<hr>
<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Choose the correct option for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The displacement-time graph of a particle moving along a straight line is a curve. The slope of the tangent drawn at any point on this graph represents:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Instantaneous acceleration</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Instantaneous velocity</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Average speed</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Total distance covered</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The ratio of the magnitude of displacement to the total distance covered by a moving body is always:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Equal to 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Greater than 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Less than or equal to 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Greater than or equal to 1</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The position  x  of a particle moving along a straight line varies with time  t  according to the equation  x = 2t<sup>3</sup> - 3t<sup>2</sup> + 5  (where  x  is in meters and  t  is in seconds). The acceleration of the particle at  t = 2 s  is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  18 m/s<sup>2</sup> </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  12 m/s<sup>2</sup> </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  24 m/s<sup>2</sup> </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  6 m/s<sup>2</sup> </li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A stone is dropped freely from the top of a tower and reaches the ground in  4 seconds . The height of the tower is (taking  g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup> ):</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  19.6 m </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  39.2 m </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  78.4 m </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  156.8 m </li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Which of the following graphs is physically impossible for one-dimensional motion of a particle?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) A position-time graph with a negative slope</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) A position-time graph where a single instant of time corresponds to two different positions</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A velocity-time graph with a constant positive slope</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) An acceleration-time graph parallel to the time axis</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with the correct scientific terms or values.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> The area bounded by the velocity-time graph and the time axis represents the total _______ of the moving object during that time interval.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> If a particle moves with a uniform velocity along a straight path, its instantaneous acceleration is equal to _______.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Select the correct option from (a), (b), (c), and (d) based on the statements given below.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> Assertion (A): A body can have zero velocity at a particular instant and yet experience a non-zero acceleration at that same instant.
Reason (R): At the highest point of its vertical path, a body thrown vertically upward momentarily comes to rest while acceleration due to gravity continues to act downwards.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Distinguish between distance and displacement by stating any two fundamental points of difference.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> A car covers the first half of the total distance between two locations at a speed of  40 km/h  and the remaining half distance at a speed of  60 km/h . Calculate the average speed of the car for the entire journey.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> Define instantaneous velocity. Write its mathematical expression using differential calculus notation.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section E: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions with complete steps and derivations.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> Using the velocity-time graph for a uniformly accelerated body shown below, derive the kinematic equation of motion for displacement:  s = ut + <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2</span></span>at<sup>2</sup> .</div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> A ball is thrown vertically upward with an initial velocity of  20 m/s  from the top of a multi-storey building of height  25 m . Taking  g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup> :
(i) Calculate the maximum height reached by the ball above the point of launch.
(ii) Find the total time elapsed before the ball hits the ground.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section F: Long Answer Question</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer all parts of the following question clearly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Answer the following multi-part question:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Draw position-time (x-t) graphs for: (i) an object moving with uniform positive velocity, (ii) an object moving with uniform negative velocity, and (iii) an object at rest.</li><li>Two trains A and B, each of length  400 m , are moving on two parallel tracks with a uniform speed of  72 km/h  in the same direction, with train A ahead of train B. The driver of train B decides to overtake train A and accelerates at  1 m/s<sup>2</sup> . If after  50 s , the guard of train B just brushes past the driver of train A, calculate the original distance between the two trains.</li></ol>
</body>
</html>